1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,660
Here's a strange fact.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,950
A language model with 70 billion parameters

3
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:08,960
needs about 140 gigabytes of memory, just to sit there.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,420
A good graphics card has 24.

5
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:14,480
And yet, people run models like this on laptops.

6
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:16,980
The trick is called quantization.

7
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:21,460
And at its heart, it's something you learned in primary school. Rounding.

8
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,320
Before any math, notice that you round things all the time.

9
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:29,070
A price of $3.99 is basically $4.

10
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:32,270
A drive of 52.7 kilometres is about 50.

11
00:00:32,310 --> 00:00:36,060
You lose a little detail, and you keep the meaning.

12
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:41,130
Computers do this too. A smooth colour gradient can use millions of shades.

13
00:00:41,170 --> 00:00:44,340
Squeeze it down to just 16, and you see a few steps,

14
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:49,220
but it's clearly the same picture. Rougher, yes, and much, much smaller.

15
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:54,710
Quantization does exactly this, to the numbers inside an AI model.

16
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:57,110
So, what numbers are we talking about?

17
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:00,920
Underneath, a neural network is a giant pile of numbers called weights.

18
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:05,120
Each one is a decimal, like 0.31, or −1.2.

19
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:11,070
When the model thinks, it mostly multiplies its inputs by these weights and adds them up.

20
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:12,570
Billions of times.

21
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:18,610
So the size of a model is simply the number of weights, times the space each one takes.

22
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:22,800
And space is measured in bits. Think of a bit as a light switch.

23
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:27,920
One switch has 2 positions, off or on, so it can name 2 different values.

24
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:32,940
Add a second switch, and you get 4 combinations. 3 switches, 8.

25
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:35,770
Every new switch doubles the count.

26
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:39,360
So with b bits, you can name 2ᵇ different values.

27
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,500
4 bits gives 16. 8 bits, 256.

28
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:46,440
16 bits, about 65,000.

29
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:51,910
Keep this doubling in mind. Every bit you remove halves your choices.

30
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:56,560
The standard way to store a weight, called FP32, uses 32 bits:

31
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:01,980
one for the sign, 8 for the size, and 23 for the fine detail.

32
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,360
That's far more precision than a model really needs.

33
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:10,200
FP16 uses half as many bits, and it's what most models ship in today.

34
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:15,060
But what if we went further, and stored each weight as a small whole number?

35
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:19,860
INT8 uses 8 bits, so 256 possible values.

36
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,760
INT4 uses 4 bits, just 16.

37
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,360
70 billion weights at 4 bits each is about 35 gigabytes. Suddenly, it fits.

38
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,240
So here's the core idea.

39
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:35,430
Picture all your weights as dots on a number line. They can sit anywhere.

40
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:39,100
Now lay down a ruler with only a few tick marks,

41
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:42,560
say 16, evenly spaced across the range.

42
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,320
Quantization means snapping every dot to its nearest tick.

43
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:51,070
Instead of storing the exact value, you store which tick it landed on,

44
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:53,680
a small integer from zero to 15.

45
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,740
That's it. That's the whole principle.

46
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:01,760
Everything else is about choosing the ruler well.

47
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:05,390
Let's do one by hand. Here are 6 weights.

48
00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:08,320
The smallest is −1, the largest is 2.

49
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:12,590
We have 4 bits, so 16 ticks, numbered 0 to 15.

50
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:16,540
Between the first tick and the last, there are 15 gaps.

51
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:19,840
The full range is 2 − (−1), which is 3.

52
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:24,060
3, shared across 15 gaps, gives a step of 0.2.

53
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:27,600
That step is called the scale, written s.

54
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:33,260
It answers one question: how much real value does one integer step stand for?

55
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:35,340
Next, where does 0 go?

56
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:40,000
Our ruler starts at −1, so real 0 sits 5 steps up.

57
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:46,040
−1, divided by 0.2, is −5. Flip the sign, and you get 5.

58
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,680
That number is the zero-point, z.

59
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:52,390
It's the integer that stands for the real value 0.

60
00:03:52,430 --> 00:03:56,310
Keeping 0 exact matters, because networks are full of zeros,

61
00:03:56,350 --> 00:04:00,410
and we never want nothing to quietly turn into something.

62
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:04,600
Now the recipe. To quantize a weight x, divide by the scale,

63
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:08,100
round to the nearest whole number, then add the zero-point.

64
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:11,390
q = round(x / s) + z.

65
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:16,520
Take 0.51. Divided by 0.2, that's 2.55.

66
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:19,520
Round it, 3. Add 5, 8.

67
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:22,720
So 0.51 is stored as the integer 8.

68
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:23,990
Let's check two more.

69
00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:29,160
−0.43, divided by 0.2, is −2.15.

70
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:32,230
Round it, −2. Add 5, 3.

71
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:36,990
And 1.37, divided by 0.2, is 6.85.

72
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:39,460
Round it, 7. Add 5, 12.

73
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,080
Do this for every weight, and our 6 decimals become

74
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:48,910
0, 3, 5, 8, 12, and 15. Each fits in just 4 bits.

75
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:51,160
When the model runs, we go backwards.

76
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:55,370
Subtract the zero-point, and multiply by the scale.

77
00:04:55,410 --> 00:05:00,220
8 minus 5 is 3. 3 times 0.2 is 0.6.

78
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:05,520
We stored 0.51, and we got back 0.6. A small error.

79
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:08,460
Do it for the whole list, and we get back

80
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:14,420
−1, −0.4, 0, 0.6, 1.4, and 2.

81
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:16,220
And here's the guarantee.

82
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:18,280
Because we always round to the nearest tick,

83
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:23,620
the error can never be more than half a step. Here, that's 0.1.

84
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:27,760
Every single weight lands within 0.1 of where it started.

85
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,760
But a model has billions of weights.

86
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:32,870
Why don't all these little errors pile up into a big one?

87
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:38,180
Remember what the model does: it multiplies each input by a weight, and adds everything up.

88
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:41,100
Let's try it with our 6 weights and 6 inputs.

89
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:43,560
With the exact weights, the total is 1.

90
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,520
With the rounded weights, the total is also 1.

91
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:47,890
Look at the errors.

92
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:51,900
Two of the products came out a little too high, one came out a little too low,

93
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:55,420
and they cancelled out. Here they cancel perfectly.

94
00:05:55,460 --> 00:05:58,840
In a real model they cancel most of the time, not always.

95
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:02,110
But the model doesn't need every weight to be perfect.

96
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:05,560
It needs the sums to be about right. That's why it barely notices.

97
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:08,440
What we just did is called asymmetric quantization.

98
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:13,920
The range can be lopsided, −1 to 2, and the zero-point shifts to match.

99
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:17,280
Its simpler cousin is symmetric quantization.

100
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:22,270
Balance the range around 0, from minus the biggest value to plus the biggest value,

101
00:06:22,310 --> 00:06:24,210
and pin the zero-point at 0.

102
00:06:24,250 --> 00:06:27,870
With 4 bits, we'd use the integers −7 to 7.

103
00:06:27,910 --> 00:06:33,640
Our biggest value is 2, so the scale is 2 divided by 7, about 0.29.

104
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:36,880
Now the formula is just q = round(x / s).

105
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:39,560
Less bookkeeping, faster math,

106
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:43,740
but the ticks below −1 are wasted, because no weight lives there.

107
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:47,220
Weights are usually balanced, so they tend to go symmetric.

108
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:54,110
Activations, the values flowing through the network, are often lopsided, so they often go asymmetric.

109
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:57,160
Now, the real enemy: outliers.

110
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:03,120
Imagine most weights live between −1 and 1, but one strange weight is 10.

111
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:07,390
Our ruler now has to stretch from −1 all the way to 10.

112
00:07:07,430 --> 00:07:13,510
That's a range of 11, split into 15 steps, so each step is about 0.73.

113
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:18,340
Every ordinary weight between −1 and 1 now lands on one of just 3 ticks.

114
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:24,550
0.1, 0.2, and 0.3 all become exactly the same number.

115
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:27,810
One outlier, and almost all the precision is gone.

116
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:32,610
And large language models really do have outliers like this.

117
00:07:32,650 --> 00:07:34,780
The fix is to use more rulers.

118
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:40,220
Per-tensor quantization uses one scale for an entire layer, so one outlier hurts everybody.

119
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:44,040
Per-channel gives every row of the weight matrix its own scale,

120
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:46,820
so the outlier only hurts its own row.

121
00:07:46,860 --> 00:07:52,920
Per-group goes further, with a fresh scale for every small block, often 128 weights.

122
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:57,830
Now an outlier only ruins its own little neighbourhood, and everyone else keeps a fine ruler.

123
00:07:57,870 --> 00:08:00,780
The price is storing those extra scales.

124
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:05,250
For groups of 128, that's only about ⅛ of a bit more per weight.

125
00:08:05,290 --> 00:08:09,630
A tiny cost, and a big part of what makes four-bit models actually work.

126
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:13,440
There's also the question of when to quantize.

127
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:18,680
Post-training quantization, or PTQ, takes a finished model and converts it.

128
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:22,080
For the weights that's easy, because we can just look at them.

129
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:27,140
Activations change with every input, so we run a few hundred sample sentences through the model

130
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:31,590
and record their typical ranges. That's called calibration.

131
00:08:31,630 --> 00:08:34,840
PTQ is quick, and needs no retraining.

132
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:40,280
Quantization-aware training, or QAT, fakes the rounding during training itself,

133
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:42,790
so the model learns to live with it.

134
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:46,800
More work, but better accuracy at very low bits.

135
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:50,600
You also choose what to quantize. Just the weights, which saves memory.

136
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:57,060
Or weights and activations together, which also lets the hardware use fast integer math.

137
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,370
You'll find these ideas inside popular tools.

138
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:03,360
GPTQ quantizes one column at a time,

139
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:07,440
and nudges the remaining weights to cancel out the error it just made.

140
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:12,900
AWQ notices that a few weights matter far more, because they meet large activations,

141
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:14,100
and protects them before rounding.

142
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:21,600
GGUF, the format used by llama.cpp, stores per-group, 4 and 5 bit weights,

143
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:24,080
so models run on ordinary laptops.

144
00:09:24,120 --> 00:09:29,960
And bitsandbytes lets you load a model in 8 or 4 bits with a single line of code.

145
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,290
So what do you actually gain?

146
00:09:32,330 --> 00:09:37,150
Going from 16 bits to 8 halves the memory, with almost no loss in quality.

147
00:09:37,190 --> 00:09:40,520
4 bits cuts it to a quarter, usually with a small drop.

148
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:46,090
Below that, at 3 or 2 bits, quality falls off fast, unless you use special tricks.

149
00:09:46,130 --> 00:09:48,520
Smaller weights also mean faster answers,

150
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:51,820
because the bottleneck is often just moving numbers out of memory.

151
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:53,920
And that means lower cost, less energy,

152
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:56,760
and models that run on your own phone, offline and private.

153
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:58,440
Let's put it all together.

154
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:03,800
Pick a range, and split it into steps. That's your scale.

155
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:07,820
Mark where zero lives. That's your zero-point.

156
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:14,640
Divide, round, shift, and store small integers. Multiply back when you need them.

157
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:18,680
The error is at most half a step, the errors mostly cancel,

158
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:25,050
and with smart choices about ranges, groups, and outliers, the model hardly feels it.

159
00:10:25,090 --> 00:10:27,390
Quantization is rounding, done cleverly.

160
00:10:27,430 --> 00:10:33,430
A little precision, traded for a model you can actually run.

